also win earlier by picking a larger number on her first move if \u03bb > sqrt(2). But for \u03bb \u2208 (1, sqrt(2)], she cannot win immediately, but she can force a win in a few rounds.\n\n- For \u03bb = 1, the sequence a_k stays constant at 1 (since a_1 = 1, a_2 = 2 - sqrt(2 - 1) = 2 - 1 = 1). So the game can go forever with both players playing the tight strategy (Alice picks a_k = 1 each odd turn, Bazza picks b_k = sqrt(2 - 1) = 1 each even turn). Indeed, if \u03bb = 1, Alice can pick x_{odd} = 1 each time (ensuring sum constraint S_{odd} = \u03bb n = n). Bazza can pick x_{even} = 1 each time (ensuring sum of squares Q_{even} = n). So the game goes forever. So for \u03bb = 1, neither player has a winning strategy (draw). However, we need to check if Bazza could have a different strategy to force a win for \u03bb = 1? Since we have a stable equilibrium where both players can maintain constraints forever, and any deviation by Alice (e.g., picking a smaller number) would reduce S_{odd} and allow Bazza to increase his numbers? Actually if Alice picks less than 1, then S_{odd} < n, leaving slack for Bazza's sum-of-squares? But Bazza's sum-of-squares constraint is independent of sum constraint. However, if Alice picks less than 1, then a_1 < \u03bb = 1, so a_2 = 2 \u03bb - sqrt(2 - a_1^2) = 2 - sqrt(2 - a_1^2) > 2 - sqrt(2) \u2248 0.5858? Actually if a_1 < 1, then a_2 > a_1? Let's check: For a_1 = 0.5, a_2 = 2 - sqrt(2 - 0.25) = 2 - sqrt(1.75) \u2248 2 - 1.3229 = 0.6771 > 0.5. So the sequence tends to 1. So if Alice picks smaller numbers, the sequence will increase towards 1. So she cannot be forced to exceed sqrt(2). So the game can go forever.\n\nThus for \u03bb = 1, the game can be infinite with optimal play. So neither player has a winning strategy. So \u03bb = 1 is a draw.\n\nNow consider \u03bb \u2208 (sqrt(2)/2, 1). In this region, the recurrence converges to a_- = \u03bb - sqrt(1 - \u03bb^2). Since a_- < sqrt(2), the sequence stays bounded below sqrt(2). So A