he odd move) is already > n, making it impossible for Bazza to choose any nonnegative x_{n+1} (even) because Q_{n+1} = Q_n + x_{n+1}^2 must be ≤ n+1. Actually, careful: At even turn n, Bazza must choose x_n such that Q_n ≤ n. So after Alice's move at odd n-1, we have Q_{n-1} ≤ n-1 (since previous even turn satisfied). Then Alice chooses x_{n} (odd) and Q_n = Q_{n-1} + x_n^2. For Bazza to be able to move at next even turn (n+1), we need Q_{n+1} ≤ n+1. But the condition for Bazza's move at even n is Q_n ≤ n. Actually, the condition is applied at the moment of his move: At even n, after he chooses x_n, the sum of squares up to n must be ≤ n. So the condition is Q_n ≤ n for each even n after his move. So for Bazza to be forced to lose, we need that after Alice's odd move (say at turn 2k-1), the sum of squares Q_{2k-1} > 2k-1? No, because the condition is only required at even n. So if Q_{2k-1} > 2k-1, that does not directly cause a loss because the condition is not required at odd turns. However, at the next even turn (2k), Bazza must choose x_{2k} such that Q_{2k} = Q_{2k-1} + x_{2k}^2 ≤ 2k. If Q_{2k-1} > 2k, then even with x_{2k}=0, Q_{2k} > 2k, so it's impossible. So to force Bazza to lose, Alice must make Q_{2k-1} > 2k (strictly greater than the next even number). Actually, if Q_{2k-1} > 2k, then even with x_{2k}=0, Q_{2k} = Q_{2k-1} > 2k, violating the condition. So the threshold is Q_{odd} > next even index.\n\nThus Alice wants to make Q_{odd} > (odd index + 1). Since odd index = 2k-1, next even = 2k. So she wants Q_{2k-1} > 2k. That is Q_{odd} > (odd index + 1). Similarly, Bazza wants to force Alice to lose: He wants S_{even} > λ (even index). Actually, Alice's constraint is S_n ≤ λ n for odd n. At even n, after Bazza's move, the sum S_n is not required to satisfy any condition. However, at the next odd turn (n+1), Alice must choose x_{n+1} such that S_{n+1} = S_n + x_{n+1} ≤ λ (n+1). So if S_n > λ (n+1), then even with x_{n+1}=0, S_{n+1} > λ (n+1), violating. So Bazza wants to make S_{even} > λ (next odd index). So he wants S_{2k} > λ (2k+1). So if he can achieve that, Alice cannot move.\n\nThus the game reduces to a competition: Alice tries to make Q_{odd} > next even index; Bazza tries to make S_{even} > λ (next odd index).\n\nNow, we need to see thresholds.\n\nFirst, consider the trivial \"zero\" strategy: Alice chooses x_{odd}=0 always. Then S_n = sum of even terms only. Bazza chooses x_{even} as large as possible while respecting Q constraint.\n\nIf Alice chooses zero on odd turns, then the sum of squares after each even turn is Q_{2k} = sum_{i=1}^k b_i^2, where b_i = x_{2i} are chosen by Bazza. The constraint Q_{2k} ≤ 2k. So Bazza can choose each b_i up to sqrt(2), because if he chooses b_i = sqrt(2) each time, then Q_{2k} = k * 2 = 2k, which meets the constraint exactly. So the maximum possible b_i is sqrt(2) each even turn, assuming previous squares sum is less than the bound. So the best for Bazza is to choose b_i = sqrt(2) each even turn. That yields Q_{2k} = 2k, Q_{2k-1} = Q_{2k-2} + 0^2 = 2(k-1). So after odd turn (2k-1), Q_{2k-1} = 2(k-1). Then the next even turn's bound is 2k. So Q_{2k-1} = 2(k-1) ≤ 2k - 2 < 2k, so not exceeding. So Alice cannot force a win because Q_{odd} = 2(k-1) < 2k. So she cannot exceed the threshold.\n\nThus with zero strategy, Alice cannot win. But does this give Bazza a winning strategy? Not necessarily; it just shows that Alice cannot force a win. For Bazza to have a winning strategy, he must be able to force Alice to lose. That is, he must be able to make S_{even} > λ (2k+1) at some point.\n\nIf Alice chooses zero on odd turns, S_{2k} = sum_{i=1}^k b_i, where b_i are the even terms. If Bazza chooses b_i = sqrt(2) each time, then S_{2k} = k sqrt(2). So S_{2k} = k sqrt(2). He wants to exceed λ (2k+1). So we need k sqrt(2) > λ (2k+1) for some k. That is sqrt(2) > λ (2 + 1/k). As k grows large, the RHS tends to 2λ. Actually, let's solve: k sqrt(2) > λ (2k+1) => sqrt(2) > λ (2 + 1/k). As