b_i.\n\nThus a_k ≤ λ (2k-1) - S_{2k-2}.\n\nSimilarly, Bazza's move b_k must satisfy:\n\nQ_{2k-1} + b_k^2 ≤ 2k.\n\nThus b_k ≤ sqrt(2k - Q_{2k-1}).\n\nThus each player can choose any nonnegative number up to these bounds.\n\nThus the game is like each player can choose any number up to a bound determined by previous moves.\n\nThus the game is reminiscent of a \"resource allocation\" game.\n\nNow, the solution's strategies:\n\n- For λ sqrt(2)/2, they propose Alice plays zero always. Then S_{odd} = 0, S_{even} = b_k sum. But they claim S_{2k} = k sqrt(2). Let's verify: If Alice always chooses zero, then S_{2k-1} = sum_{i=1}^{k-1} b_i. After each even turn, S_{2k} = sum_{i=1}^{k} b_i. If b_i = sqrt(2) each time, then S_{2k} = k sqrt(2). Indeed.\n\nNow we need to check if this is a valid strategy for Bazza (i.e., b_k = sqrt(2) is always allowed). At turn 2k, Q_{2k-1} = sum_{i=1}^{k-1} b_i^2 = (k-1) * 2 = 2(k-1). So the bound for b_k is sqrt(2k - Q_{2k-1}) = sqrt(2k - 2(k-1)) = sqrt(2) indeed. So b_k = sqrt(2) is allowed. So Bazza can always pick sqrt(2). So the game can continue forever. However the solution claims that this shows Bazza has a winning strategy for λ sqrt(2)/2. Wait, they claim \"Bazza has a winning strategy\" but they only show that the game can go forever (draw). Actually they say \"Thus the game can be continued forever; in particular Bazza has a strategy that prevents Alice from ever winning, i.e. Bazza has a winning strategy for λ sqrt(2)/2\". This is a bit ambiguous: \"winning strategy\" is defined as a strategy that ensures the player wins (i.e., the opponent cannot move). But they claim that the strategy ensures the opponent never wins, i.e., the game goes forever. The problem statement says: \"Determine all values of λ for which Alice has a winning strategy and all those for which Bazza has a winning strategy.\" It also says: \"If the game goes forever, neither player wins.\" So a \"winning strategy\" means a strategy that guarantees a win (i.e., t