keep b_i small, we consider b_i = 0. So we need sum_{i=1}^{k} a_i^2 > 2k.\n\nThus the condition for Alice to force a win is: There exists k such that sum_{i=1}^{k} a_i^2 > 2k, while sum_{i=1}^{k} a_i ≤ λ (2k-1). Since she can choose a_i adaptively, we need to see if she can achieve this for some λ.\n\nNow, we can consider the worst-case scenario for Alice: She wants to maximize sum of squares given a bound on sum. For a given total sum T, the maximum sum of squares is achieved by concentrating all mass into a single term: i.e., one a_i = T, others zero, giving sum of squares = T^2. However she must also respect that a_i ≤ something? There's no explicit upper bound on a_i besides the constraint that after her move, S_{odd} ≤ λ n. So she could choose a huge a_i as long as the sum stays within bound. So she could concentrate all sum into a single a_i at some turn.\n\nThus we can consider a strategy where Alice, at some odd turn, uses all remaining allowed sum to make a huge a_i, making its square exceed the bound for Q. But she must also consider that the sum of squares includes previous a_i's and b_i's. If she hasn't used any previous a_i's, she could at turn n = 2k-1 choose a_k = λ (2k-1) (i.e., fill the entire allowed sum). Then sum of squares = (λ (2k-1))^2. She wants this > 2k. So we need (λ (2k-1))^2 > 2k. Solve for λ: λ > sqrt(2k) / (2k-1). As k grows, sqrt(2k) / (2k-1) ~ sqrt(2/k) / (2 - 1/k) ~ sqrt(2/k) / 2 = sqrt(2/k) / 2 = (1/2) sqrt(2/k) = sqrt(2)/(2 sqrt(k)). As k → ∞, this tends to 0. So for any λ > 0, for sufficiently large k, λ (2k-1) can be large enough? Wait, λ (2k-1) grows linearly with k, so its square grows quadratically ~ λ^2 (2k)^2 = 4 λ^2 k^2. Compare to 2k, which is linear. So for any λ > 0, eventually λ (2k-1) squared > 2k for large enough k. So Alice could potentially force a win for any λ > 0? That seems contradictory to the solution's claim that only λ > 1 yields a win for Alice. So there must be a subtlety: Alice cannot concentrate all sum