tay in \\([0,\\sqrt2]\\), so neither player can force a win; the game can be played forever. Moreover any deviation from the greedy moves only helps the opponent, so neither player possesses a winning strategy.  \n\n**Final answer:**  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\begin{aligned}\n&\\text{Alice has a winning strategy }\\iff\\ \\lambda>1,\\\\[2mm]\n&\\text{Bazza has a winning strategy }\\iff\\ \\lambda<\\dfrac1{\\sqrt2},\\\\[2mm]\n&\\text{For }\\dfrac1{\\sqrt2}\\le\\lambda\\le1\\text{ the game can last forever (no winning strategy).}\n\\end{aligned}}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n**### Detailed Solution**\n\n#### 1.  Formalisation of the game  \n\nFor each turn \\(n\\) let  \n\n\\[\nT_n:=\\sum_{i=1}^{n}x_i\\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad \nS_n:=\\sum_{i=1}^{n}x_i^{2}.\n\\]\n\nThe rules are  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{cases}\n\\text{if }n\\text{ is odd: }T_n\\le\\lambda n,\\\\[1mm]\n\\text{if }n\\text{ is even: }S_n\\le n .\n\\end{cases}\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nIf a player cannot choose a non‑negative \\(x_n\\) satisfying the appropriate inequality, the game stops and the opponent wins.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n#### 2.  Optimal moves  \n\n*Alice (odd turn).*  \nAt an odd turn \\(n=2k+1\\) the quantity \\(T_{2k}\\) is already fixed.  \nThe admissible values are  \n\n\\[\n0\\le x_{2k+1}\\le \\lambda(2k+1)-T_{2k}.\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(x_{2k+1}\\) contributes \\(x_{2k+1}^{2}\\) to the sum of squares, Alice maximises the chance that Bazza will later be unable to satisfy his inequality by taking the **largest** admissible value, i.e.\n\n\\[\nx_{2k+1}= \\lambda(2k+1)-T_{2k}.\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n*Bazza (even turn).*  \nAt an even turn \\(n=2k\\) the quantity \\(S_{2k-1}\\) is already fixed.  \nThe admissible values are  \n\n\\[\n0\\le x_{2k}\\le\\sqrt{\\,2k-S_{2k-1}\\,}.\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(x_{2k}\\) adds directly to the ordinary sum \\(T_{2k}\\), Bazza maximises the restriction on Alice’s next move by taking the **largest** admissible value:\n\n\\[\nx_{2k}= \\sqrt{\\,2k-S_{2k-1}\\,}.\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nAny smaller choice would leave more slack for the opponent and cannot improve a player’s chance to win. Hence (3)–(5) describe optimal play for both players.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n#### 3.  Reduction to a one‑dimensional recurrence  \n\nDefine the odd–turn values  \n\n\\[\na_k:=x_{2k+1}\\qquad(k\\ge0),\n\\]\n\nand the even–turn values  \n\n\\[\ny_k:=x_{2k}\\qquad(k\\ge1).\n\\]\n\nFrom (5) and the fact that after an odd move the sum of squares equals\n\\(S_{2k-1}=2(k-1)+a_k^{2}\\) (the even turns always saturate the bound \\(S_{2k}=2k\\)),\nwe obtain  \n\n\\[\ny_k=\\sqrt{\\,2-a_k^{2}\\,}\\qquad(k\\ge1). \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nFrom (3) we have  \n\n\\[\na_{k}= \\lambda(2k+1)-T_{2k}\n      =\\lambda(2k+1)-\\bigl(\\lambda(2k-1)+y_k\\bigr)\n      =2\\lambda-y_k\\qquad(k\\ge1). \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nThe initial odd move is forced by (3) with \\(T_0=0\\):\n\n\\[\na_0=x_1=\\lambda. \\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nCombining (6)–(8) yields the autonomous recurrence  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{a_{k+1}=f(a_k),\\qquad \n      f(a)=2\\lambda-\\sqrt{\\,2-a^{2}\\,},\\qquad a\\in[0,\\sqrt2].} \\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nThe game proceeds as long as every term of the sequence stays in the interval\n\\([0,\\sqrt2]\\).  \n\n*If for some \\(k\\) we obtain \\(a_k>\\sqrt2\\), then after Alice’s move the sum of\nsquares exceeds the bound for the next even turn, so Bazza cannot move and\nAlice wins.*\n\n*If for some \\(k\\) we obtain \\(a_k<0\\), then Alice cannot make the required odd\nmove, so Bazza wins.*\n\nThus the fate of the game is completely determined by the dynamics of (9).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n#### 4.  Elementary properties of the map \\(f\\)\n\nFor a fixed \\(\\lambda\\ge0\\) define  \n\n\\[\nf(a)=2\\lambda-\\sqrt{2-a^{2}},\\qquad a\\in[0,\\sqrt2].\n\\]\n\n*Monotonicity and convexity.*  \n\\[\nf'(a)=\\frac{a}{\\sqrt{2-a^{2}}}\\ge0,\\qquad \nf''(a)=\\frac{2}{(2-a^{2})^{3/2}}>0,\n\\]\nso \\(f\\) is strictly increasing and convex on \\([0,\\sqrt2]\\).\n\n*Fixed points.*  \n\\(a=f(a)\\) is equivalent to  \n\n\\[\n\\sqrt{2-a^{2}}=2\\lambda-a\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad\na^{2}-2\\lambda a+2\\lambda^{2}-1=0,\n\\]\ni.e.  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{(a-\\lambda)^{2}=1-\\lambda^{2}}.\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nHence\n\n\\[\na_{\\pm}= \\lambda\\pm\\sqrt{1-\\lambda^{2}}. \\tag{11}\n\\]\n\nConsequences:\n\n* If \\(\\lambda>1\\) the right‑hand side of (10) is negative; there is **no**\nreal fixed point in \\([0,\\sqrt2]\\).\n\n* If \\(\\lambda=1\\) the only fixed point is \\(a=1\\).\n\n* If \\(0\\le\\lambda<1\\) there are two real fixed points \\(a_{+}>a_{-}\\).  \nThe lower one satisfies \\(a_{-}\\ge0\\) **iff** \\(\\lambda\\ge\\frac1{\\sqrt2}\\) (because\n\\(a_{-}= \\lambda-\\sqrt{1-\\lambda^{2}}\\ge0\\iff\\lambda^{2}\\ge\\frac12\\)).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n#### 5.  Behaviour of the orbit \\((a_k)\\)\n\nWe study the orbit of (9) according to the value of \\(\\lambda\\).\n\n---\n\n##### 5.1  The case \\(\\boldsymbol{\\lambda>1}\\)\n\nFor any \\(a\\in[0,\\sqrt2]\\),\n\n\\[\ng(a):=f(a)-a=2\\lambda-a-\\sqrt{2-a^{2}}.\n\\]\n\nSince \\(g\\) is increasing (its derivative is \\(g'(a)=\\frac{a}{\\sqrt{2-a^{2}}}>0\\))\nand  \n\n\\[\ng(\\lambda)=\\lambda-\\sqrt{2-\\lambda^{2}}>0\n\\quad(\\lambda>1\\Rightarrow\\lambda^{2}>1\\Rightarrow\\sqrt{2-\\lambda^{2}}<1),\n\\]\n\nwe have \\(g(a)>0\\) for all \\(a\\in[\\lambda,\\sqrt2)\\); consequently  \n\n\\[\nf(a)>a\\qquad\\text{for all }a\\in[\\lambda,\\sqrt2). \\tag{12}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(a_0=\\lambda\\) and \\(f\\) is increasing, (12) yields  \n\n\\[\na_0<a_1<a_2<\\dots<a_k<\\sqrt2\\quad\\text{as long as }a_k<\\sqrt2.\n\\tag{13}\n\\]\n\nThe sequence \\((a_k)\\) is strictly increasing and bounded above by \\(\\sqrt2\\);\nhence it converges to a limit \\(L\\le\\sqrt2\\).  \nIf \\(L<\\sqrt2\\) then \\(L\\) would be a fixed point of \\(f\\), contradicting the\nabsence of fixed points for \\(\\lambda>1\\).  Therefore \\(L=\\sqrt2\\).\n\nSince \\(a_k\\to\\sqrt2\\), we have \\(\\sqrt{2-a_k^{2}}\\to0\\).  From the recurrence\n\\(a_{k+1}=2\\lambda-\\sqrt{2-a_k^{2}}\\) we obtain  \n\n\\[\n\\lim_{k\\to\\infty}a_{k+1}=2\\lambda>\\sqrt2.\n\\]\n\nHence there exists a finite index \\(N\\) with \\(a_N>\\sqrt2\\).  At that moment\nBazza’s next even turn is impossible, so **Alice wins**.\n\n---\n\n##### 5.2  The case \\(\\boldsymbol{0\\le\\lambda<\\frac1{\\sqrt2}}\\)\n\nHere the lower fixed point \\(a_{-}\\) given by (11) is negative.\nFor any \\(a\\in(0,\\sqrt2]\\),\n\n\\[\ng(a)=f(a)-a=2\\lambda-a-\\sqrt{2-a^{2}}<0,\n\\]\nbecause now \\(\\lambda<1\\) implies \\(\\lambda-\\sqrt{2-\\lambda^{2}}<0\\) and\n\\(g\\) is increasing.  Consequently  \n\n\\[\nf(a)<a\\qquad\\text{for all }a\\in(0,\\sqrt2]. \\tag{14}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(a_0=\\lambda>0\\) and \\(f\\) is increasing, (14) yields  \n\n\\[\na_0>a_1>a_2>\\dots>a_k>0\\quad\\text{as long as }a_k>0. \\tag{15}\n\\]\n\nThus \\((a_k)\\) is a strictly decreasing sequence bounded below by the (negative)\nfixed point \\(a_{-}\\).  Hence it must cross zero after finitely many steps:\nthere exists \\(N\\) with \\(a_N\\ge0\\) and \\(a_{N+1}<0\\).  At turn \\(2N+3\\) Alice\ncannot choose a non‑negative number, so **Bazza wins**.\n\n---\n\n##### 5.3  The case \\(\\boldsymbol{\\frac1{\\sqrt2}\\le\\lambda\\le1}\\)\n\nNow the lower fixed point \\(a_{-}\\) is non‑negative.  \nFrom (14) we still have \\(f(a)<a\\) for every \\(a\\in(\\lambda,\\sqrt2]\\); therefore\nthe orbit is non‑increasing:\n\n\\[\na_0\\ge a_1\\ge a_2\\ge\\dots\\ge0. \\tag{16}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(a_{-}\\) satisfies \\(f(a_{-})=a_{-}\\) and \\(a_{-}\\le a_k\\) for all \\(k\\)\n(by induction using the monotonicity of \\(f\\)), the decreasing sequence is\nbounded below by \\(a_{-}\\).  Hence it converges to a limit \\(L\\ge a_{-}\\).\nThe only possible limit points are the fixed points of \\(f\\); the decreasing\norbit cannot converge to the larger fixed point \\(a_{+}\\) (which is \\(>\\lambda\\)),\nso \\(L=a_{-}\\).  In particular every term satisfies  \n\n\\[\n0\\le a_k\\le\\sqrt2\\qquad\\text{for all }k.\n\\]\n\nThus the game can be continued forever by letting both players follow the\ngreedy moves (6)–(7).  Moreover, any deviation harms the deviating player:\nif Alice chooses a smaller odd move, the subsequent even move becomes larger,\nwhich pushes the next odd move even lower; if Bazza chooses a smaller even\nmove, the next odd move becomes larger, possibly exceeding \\(\\sqrt2\\) and\nhanding the win to Alice.  Hence **neither player possesses a winning\nstrategy** when \\(\\frac1{\\sqrt2}\\le\\lambda\\le1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n#### 6.  Conclusion\n\nCollecting the three cases we obtain the complete classification.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\n\\begin{aligned}\n&\\text{Alice has a winning strategy } \\iff\\ \\lambda>1,\\\\[2mm]\n&\\text{Bazza has a winning strategy } \\iff\\ \\lambda<\\dfrac1{\\sqrt2},\\\\[2mm]\n&\\text{For }\\dfrac1{\\sqrt2}\\le\\lambda\\le1\\text{ the game can last forever; neither player can force a win.}\n\\end{aligned}}\n\\]\n\n*Remark on the trivial first move.*  \nFor \\(n=1\\) (Alice’s first turn) the optimal move is \\(x_1=\\lambda\\); this\nchoice is admissible for every \\(\\lambda\\ge0\\) and initiates the dynamics\ndescribed above.  Hence a configuration with exactly one element always\nexists (take \\(x_1=\\lambda\\)). ∎"
