in \\([0,\\sqrt2]\\), so neither player can force a win; the game can be played forever. Moreover any deviation from the greedy moves only helps the opponent, so neither player possesses a winning strategy.  \n\n**Final answer:**  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\begin{aligned}\n&\\text{Alice has a winning strategy }\\iff\\ \\lambda>1,\\\\[2mm]\n&\\text{Bazza has a winning strategy }\\iff\\ \\lambda<\\dfrac1{\\sqrt2},\\\\[2mm]\n&\\text{For }\\dfrac1{\\sqrt2}\\le\\lambda\\le1\\text{ the game can last forever (no winning strategy).}\n\\end{aligned}}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n**### Detailed Solution**\n\n#### 1.  Formalisation of the game  \n\nFor each turn \\(n\\) let  \n\n\\[\nT_n:=\\sum_{i=1}^{n}x_i\\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad \nS_n:=\\sum_{i=1}^{n}x_i^{2}.\n\\]\n\nThe rules are  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{cases}\n\\text{if }n\\text{ is odd: }T_n\\le\\lambda n,\\\\[1mm]\n\\text{if }n\\text{ is even: }S_n\\le n .\n\\end{cases}\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nIf a player cannot choose a non‑negative \\(x_n\\) satisfying the appropriate inequality, the game stops and the opponent wins.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n#### 2.  Optimal moves  \n\n*Alice (odd turn).*  \nAt an odd turn \\(n=2k+1\\) the quantity \\(T_{2k}\\) is already fixed.  \nThe admissible values are  \n\n\\[\n0\\le x_{2k+1}\\le \\lambda(2k+1)-T_{2k}.\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(x_{2k+1}\\) contributes \\(x_{2k+1}^{2}\\) to the sum of squares, Alice maximises the chance that Bazza will later be unable to satisfy his inequality by taking the **largest** admissible value, i.e.\n\n\\[\nx_{2k+1}= \\lambda(2k+1)-T_{2k}.\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n*Bazza (even turn).*  \nAt an even turn \\(n=2k\\) the quantity \\(S_{2k-1}\\) is already fixed.  \nThe admissible values are  \n\n\\[\n0\\le x_{2k}\\le\\sqrt{\\,2k-S_{2k-1}\\,}.\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(x_{2k}\\) adds directly to the ordinary sum \\(T_{2k}\\), Bazza maximises the restriction on Alice’s next move by taking the **largest** admissible value:\n\n\\[\nx_{2k}= \\sqrt{\\,2k-S_{2k-1}\\,}.\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nAny smaller choice would leave more slack for the opponent and cannot improve a player’s chance to win. Hence (3)–(5) describe optimal play for both players.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n#### 3.  Reduction to a one‑dimensional recurrence  \n\nDefine the odd–turn values  \n\n\\[\na_k:=x_{2k+1}\\qquad(k\\ge0),\n\\]\n\nand the even–turn values  \n\n\\[\ny_k:=x_{2k}\\qquad(k\\ge1).\n\\]\n\nFrom (5) and the fact that after an odd move the sum of squares equals\n\\(S_{2k-1}=2(k-1)+a_k^{2}\\) (the even turns always saturate the bound \\(S_{2k}=2k\\)),\nwe obtain  \n\n\\[\ny_k=\\sqrt{\\,2-a_k^{2}\\,}\\qquad(k\\ge1). \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nFrom (3) we have  \n\n\\[\na_{k}= \\lambda(2k+1)-T_{2k}\n      =\\lambda(2k+1)-\\bigl(\\lambda(2k-1)+y_k\\bigr)\n      =2\\lambda-y_k\\qquad(k\\ge1). \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nThe initial odd move is forced by (3) with \\(T_0=0\\):\n\n\\[\na_0=x_1=\\lambda. \\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nCombining (6)–(8) yields the autonomous recurrence  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{a_{k+1}=f(a_k),\\qquad \n      f(a)=2\\lambda-\\sqrt{\\,2-a^{2}\\,},\\qquad a\\in[0,\\sqrt2].} \\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nThe game proceeds as long as every term of the sequence stays in the interval\n\\([0,\\sqrt2]\\).  \n\n*If for some \\(k\\) we obtain \\(a_k>\\sqrt2\\), then after Alice’s move the sum of\nsquares exceeds the bound for the next even turn, so Bazza cannot move and\nAlice wins.*\n\n*If for some \\(k\\) we obtain \\(a_k<0\\), then Alice cannot make the required odd\nmove, so Bazza wins.*\n\nThus the fate of the game is completely determined by the dynamics of (9).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n#### 4.  Elementary properties of the map \\(f\\)\n\nFor a fixed \\(\\lambda\\ge0\\) define  \n\n\\[\nf(a)=2\\lambda-\\sqrt{2-a^{2}},\\qquad a\\in[0,\\sqrt2].\n\\]\n\n*Monotonicity and convexity.*  \n\\[\nf'(a)=\\frac{a}{\\sqrt{2-a^{2}}}\\ge0,\\qquad \nf''(a)=\\frac{2}{(2-a^{2})^{3/2}}>0,\n\\]\nso