e x_3 = 0, but S_3 = x_1 + x_2 + x_3 = x_2 > 0, which violates S_3 ≤ 0. So Alice cannot move at turn 3, so she loses. So Bazza wins for λ = 0, which is < 1/√2. So consistent.\n\nNow check λ = 1/√2 ≈ 0.7071. According to solution, infinite play possible. Let's see if that seems plausible. At λ = 1/√2, we have λ^2 = 1/2. Then x_1 = λ = 1/√2, S_1 = λ, Q_1 = λ^2 = 1/2. Then x_2 can be sqrt(2 - λ^2) = sqrt(2 - 1/2) = sqrt(3/2) ≈ 1.2247. So a_2 = x_2 = sqrt(2 - λ^2) = sqrt(3/2). Then b_2 = x_3 = 2λ - a_2 = 2/√2 - sqrt(3/2) = sqrt(2) - sqrt(3/2) ≈ 1.4142 - 1.2247 = 0.1895 > 0. So still positive. Then a_3 = sqrt(2 - (2λ - a_2)^2) = sqrt(2 - b_2^2). Compute b_2^2 ≈ 0.0359, so a_3 ≈ sqrt(1.9641) ≈ 1.4015. Then b_3 = 2λ - a_3 = sqrt(2) - 1.4015 ≈ 1.4142 - 1.4015 = 0.0127. Then a_4 = sqrt(2 - b_3^2) ≈ sqrt(2 - 0.000162) ≈ sqrt(1.999838) ≈ 1.4141. Then b_4 = sqrt(2) - a_4 ≈ 0.0001. So b_k tends to 0, a_k tends to sqrt(2). So the sequence stays within the interval. So infinite play possible. So consistent.\n\nNow check λ = 1. According to solution, infinite play possible (since λ ∈ [1/√2,1]). Let's test: λ = 1. Then x_1 = 1, Q_1 = 1. Then x_2 = sqrt(2 - 1) = 1. So a_2 = 1. Then b_2 = 2λ - a_2 = 2 - 1 = 1. So x_3 = 1. Then Q_3 = Q_2 + x_3^2 = (x_1^2 + x_2^2) + x_3^2 = 1 + 1 + 1 = 3. But the constraint at n=3 is for Alice (odd) sum ≤ λ*3 = 3. S_3 = x_1 + x_2 + x_3 = 1 + 1 + 1 = 3, okay. At n=4 (even), we need Q_4 ≤ 4. Q_4 = Q_3 + x_4^2. x_4 = sqrt(4 - Q_3) = sqrt(4 - 3) = 1. So a_3 = x_4 = 1. Then b_3 = 2λ - a_3 = 2 - 1 = 1. So indeed the pattern repeats: all x_n = 1. So infinite play possible. So correct.\n\nNow λ > 1. For λ > √2 ≈ 1.4142, the solution says Alice wins immediately because after x_1 = λ, λ^2 > 2, so Bazza cannot choose any x_2 satisfying x_1^2 + x_2^2 ≤ 2 (since n=2). Indeed, the constraint for n=2 is Q_2 ≤ 2. Q_2 = x_1^2 + x_2^2. Since x_1^2 > 2, any nonnegative x_2^2 ≥ 0, so Q_2 > 2, violating constraint. So Bazza cannot move, so Alice wins. So correct.\n\nNow for