            "content": "**Answer**\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\begin{aligned}\n&\\text{Alice has a winning strategy  } \\Longleftrightarrow\\ \\lambda>\\sqrt 2 ,\\\\[2mm]\n&\\text{Bazza has a winning strategy } \\Longleftrightarrow\\ \\lambda<\\frac1{\\sqrt 2},\\\\[2mm]\n&\\text{for }\\frac1{\\sqrt 2}\\le \\lambda\\le\\sqrt 2\\text{ neither player can force a win\n(the game can be continued forever).}\n\\end{aligned}}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### Summary  \n\n* **Verdict.**  \n  – Alice wins exactly for \\(\\lambda>\\sqrt2\\).  \n  – Bazza wins exactly for \\(\\lambda<1/\\sqrt2\\).  \n  – For \\(\\displaystyle\\frac1{\\sqrt2}\\le\\lambda\\le\\sqrt2\\) neither player has a winning\n    strategy; with optimal play the game can be made to last forever.\n\n* **Method sketch.**  \n  1.  Write \\(S_n=\\sum_{i=1}^{n}x_i\\) and \\(Q_n=\\sum_{i=1}^{n}x_i^{2}\\).  \n      Alice loses at an odd turn \\(n\\) iff \\(S_{n-1}>\\lambda n\\);  \n      Bazza loses at an even turn \\(n\\) iff \\(Q_n>n\\).\n\n  2.  **Bazza’s maximal sum.**  \n      If Alice always chooses \\(x_{2k-1}=0\\), then only Bazza’s even moves\n      contribute to the sum.  With the square‑budget \\(Q_{2k}\\le 2k\\) the\n      Cauchy–Schwarz inequality gives  \n\n      \\[\n      \\bigl(\\sum_{j=1}^{k}x_{2j}\\bigr)^{2}\\le k\\sum_{j=1}^{k}x_{2j}^{2}\\le k\\cdot2k=2k^{2},\n      \\]\n      whence \\(\\displaystyle\\sum_{j=1}^{k}x_{2j}\\le\\frac{2k}{\\sqrt2}= \\frac{2k}{\\sqrt2}\\).\n      Equality is attainable by taking every even move equal to \\(\\sqrt2\\).\n      Consequently after an even turn \\(n=2k\\)\n\n      \\[\n      S_{n}= \\frac{n}{\\sqrt2}.\n      \\tag{1}\n      \\]\n\n  3.  **Bazza’s winning region.**  \n      If \\(\\lambda<1/\\sqrt2\\), then from (1)\n\n      \\[\n      S_{2k}= \\frac{2k}{\\sqrt2}> \\lambda(2k+1)\n      \\]\n\n      for all sufficiently large \\(k\\); therefore Alice cannot satisfy her\n      inequality on the next odd turn and loses.  The strategy “play\n      \\(x_{2k}=\\sqrt2\\) for every even \\(k\\)” is a winning strategy for\n      Bazza when \\(\\lambda<1/\\sqrt2\\).\n\n  4.  **Alice’s maximal contribution to the square sum.**  \n      If Bazza always chooses \\(x_{2k}=0\\), then only Alice’s odd moves affect\n      the square sum.  Because on an odd turn \\(n\\) the total sum may not exceed\n      \\(\\lambda n\\), the increment she can add is at most \\(\\lambda\\); hence\n      each odd move contributes at most \\(\\lambda^{2}\\) to \\(Q\\).  After an even\n      turn \\(n=2k\\),\n\n      \\[\n      Q_{n}\\le k\\lambda^{2}.\n      \\tag{2}\n      \\]\n\n      Equality is attained by taking every odd move equal to \\(\\lambda\\).\n\n  5.  **Alice’s winning region.**  \n      If \\(\\lambda>\\sqrt2\\) then \\(\\lambda^{2}>2\\) and (2) gives  \n\n      \\[\n      Q_{2k}=k\\lambda^{2}>2k=n .\n      \\]\n\n      Thus Bazza cannot keep his square sum within the allowed bound; he loses\n      at the first even turn.  The strategy “play \\(x_{2k-1}=\\lambda\\) on every\n      odd turn” is a winning strategy for Alice when \\(\\lambda>\\sqrt2\\).\n\n  6.  **The intermediate interval.**  \n      *For \\(\\lambda\\ge 1/\\sqrt2\\):* Alice can avoid losing by always playing\n      \\(x_{2k-1}=0\\); then the sum never exceeds \\(\\lambda n\\) because the\n      maximal sum (1) satisfies \\(S_{2k}\\le\\lambda(2k+1)\\).\n\n      *For \\(\\lambda\\le\\sqrt2\\):* Bazza can avoid losing by always playing\n      \\(x_{2k}=0\\); then the square sum never exceeds the bound because\n      (2) gives \\(Q_{2k}\\le2k\\).\n\n      Hence for every \\(\\lambda\\) with  \n\n      \\[\n      \\frac1{\\sqrt2}\\le\\lambda\\le\\sqrt2\n      \\]\n\n      both players have a strategy that prevents the opponent from ever\n      violating his own condition, so the game can be continued indefinitely.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### Detailed Solution  \n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\sum_{i=1}^{n}x_i ,\\qquad Q_n=\\sum_{i=1}^{n}x_i^{2}.\n\\]\n\nThe rules are  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{cases}\n\\text{(odd }n) & S_n\\le\\lambda n,\\\\[1mm]\n\\text{(even }n) & Q_n\\le n .\n\\end{cases}\n\\tag{R}\n\\]\n\nA player loses exactly when, on his turn, the corresponding inequality of\n(R) is already violated.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  Bazza’s maximal possible sum\n\nAssume Alice always chooses \\(x_{2k-1}=0\\).  \nOnly the even moves \\(y_k:=x_{2k}\\;(k\\ge1)\\) contribute to the sum.\nFrom the square condition we have  \n\n\\[\n\\sum_{j=1}^{k}y_j^{2}=Q_{2k}\\le 2k .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nBy Cauchy–Schwarz,\n\n\\[\n\\Bigl(\\sum_{j=1}^{k}y_j\\Bigr)^{2}\\le k\\sum_{j=1}^{k}y_j^{2}\n\\le k\\cdot2k=2k^{2},\n\\]\n\nhence  \n\n\\[\n\\sum_{j=1}^{k}y_j\\le\\frac{2k}{\\sqrt2}= \\frac{2k}{\\sqrt2}.\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nEquality in (4) is attained by taking every even move equal to \\(\\sqrt2\\):\n\\(y_j=\\sqrt2\\) gives \\(\\sum y_j^{2}=2k\\) and \\(\\sum y_j= k\\sqrt2\\).\n\nThus after an even turn \\(n=2k\\),\n\n\\[\nS_{n}=k\\sqrt2=\\frac{n}{\\sqrt2}.\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Bazza’s winning region \\(\\displaystyle\\lambda<\\frac1{\\sqrt2}\\)\n\nIf \\(\\lambda<1/\\sqrt2\\), then from (5)\n\n\\[\nS_{2k}= \\frac{2k}{\\sqrt2}> \\lambda(2k+1)\n\\]\n\nfor all sufficiently large \\(k\\).  Consequently on the following odd turn\n\\(2k+1\\) Alice cannot satisfy \\(S_{2k+1}\\le\\lambda(2k+1)\\) (even with\n\\(x_{2k+1}=0\\) the inequality fails).  Hence Alice loses.\n\nThe explicit strategy for Bazza is\n\n\\[\nx_{2k}= \\sqrt2\\qquad(k=1,2,\\dots),\\qquad x_{2k-1}=0 .\n\\]\n\nIt respects his own condition because after the \\(k\\)-th even move\n\\(Q_{2k}=2k\\), exactly the allowed bound.  Therefore Bazza wins for every\n\\(\\lambda<1/\\sqrt2\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Alice’s maximal possible contribution to the square sum\n\nNow assume Bazza always chooses \\(x_{2k}=0\\).  \nOnly Alice’s odd moves \\(z_k:=x_{2k-1}\\) affect the square sum.\nOn an odd turn \\(n=2k-1\\) the sum restriction gives  \n\n\\[\n\\sum_{j=1}^{k}z_j = S_{2k-1}\\le\\lambda(2k-1).\n\\]\n\nConsequently the increment she can add at that turn is at most  \n\n\\[\nz_k\\le\\lambda .\n\\]\n\nHence each odd move contributes at most \\(\\lambda^{2}\\) to the square sum, and\nafter an even turn \\(n=2k\\)\n\n\\[\nQ_{n}= \\sum_{j=1}^{k}z_j^{2}\\le k\\lambda^{2}.\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nEquality in (6) is achieved by taking every odd move equal to \\(\\lambda\\):\n\\(z_j=\\lambda\\) gives \\(Q_{2k}=k\\lambda^{2}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Alice’s winning region \\(\\displaystyle\\lambda>\\sqrt2\\)\n\nIf \\(\\lambda>\\sqrt2\\) then \\(\\lambda^{2}>2\\).  From (6),\n\n\\[\nQ_{2k}=k\\lambda^{2}>2k=n .\n\\]\n\nThus Bazza cannot keep his square sum within the allowed bound; he loses at\nthe first even turn.  \n\nA concrete winning strategy for Alice is\n\n\\[\nx_{2k-1}= \\lambda\\qquad(k=1,2,\\dots),\\qquad x_{2k}=0 .\n\\]\n\nWe must verify that the sum condition is never violated.  \nAssume inductively that after the even turn \\(2k-2\\),\n\n\\[\nS_{2k-2}\\le\\lambda(2k-2).\n\\]\n\nAlice then adds \\(\\lambda\\), obtaining  \n\n\\[\nS_{2k-1}=S_{2k-2}+\\lambda\\le\\lambda(2k-1),\n\\]\n\nso the odd‑turn condition holds.  Bazza’s next move is \\(0\\), hence  \n\n\\[\nS_{2k}=S_{2k-1}\\le\\lambda(2k-1)\\le\\lambda(2k+1),\n\\]\n\nso the induction proceeds.  Therefore the strategy is legal and forces a win\nfor every \\(\\lambda>\\sqrt2\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  The interval \\(\\displaystyle\\frac1{\\sqrt2}\\le\\lambda\\le\\sqrt2\\)\n\n*Survival of Alice.*  \nIf \\(\\lambda\\ge 1/\\sqrt2\\) she can simply play \\(x_{2k-1}=0\\) for all odd\nturns.  Bazza’s maximal sum (5) then satisfies  \n\n\\[\nS_{2k}= \