+1} ≤ λ (2k+1). So we need k sqrt(2) ≤ λ (2k+1). For λ < sqrt(2)/2, we need to see if this inequality holds for all k. Let's test: λ (2k+1) = (2k+1) λ. If λ < sqrt(2)/2, then λ (2k+1) < (sqrt(2)/2) (2k+1) = sqrt(2) k + (sqrt(2)/2). So λ (2k+1) < sqrt(2) k + sqrt(2)/2. Compare to k sqrt(2). Since sqrt(2) k + sqrt(2)/2 > sqrt(2) k (by sqrt(2)/2 > 0), we have λ (2k+1) < sqrt(2) k + sqrt(2)/2. But we need to check if k sqrt(2) ≤ λ (2k+1). Actually we need to see if S_{2k+1} = k sqrt(2) ≤ λ (2k+1). Since λ (2k+1) < sqrt(2) k + sqrt(2)/2, we cannot guarantee that k sqrt(2) ≤ λ (2k+1). In fact, for large k, λ (2k+1) ≈ 2λ k, while k sqrt(2) ≈ sqrt(2) k. So the inequality holds if 2λ ≥ sqrt(2), i.e., λ ≥ sqrt(2)/2. If λ < sqrt(2)/2, then 2λ < sqrt(2), so eventually k sqrt(2) > λ (2k+1) for large k? Let's check: inequality k sqrt(2) ≤ λ (2k+1) is equivalent to sqrt(2) ≤ λ (2 + 1/k). As k → ∞, RHS → 2λ. So need sqrt(2) ≤ 2λ, i.e., λ ≥ sqrt(2)/2. If λ < sqrt(2)/2, then for large k, inequality fails, meaning Alice cannot maintain S_{2k+1} ≤ λ (2k+1). But the solution claims that for λ < sqrt(2)/2, Alice never violates her inequality. That seems contradictory. Let's check the solution's reasoning: They claim:\n\n\"For λ < sqrt(2)/2, ... S_{2k} = k sqrt(2). For λ < sqrt(2)/2,\n\nλ (2k+1) = (sqrt(2)/2) (2k+1) = sqrt(2) k + sqrt(2)/2 > k sqrt(2) = S_{2k}.\"\n\nBut they compare S_{2k} (even turn sum) to λ (2k+1) (odd turn bound). Actually S_{2k} is the sum after even turn, not after odd turn. At odd turn n=2k+1, S_{2k+1} = S_{2k} + x_{2k+1} (Alice's move). Since Alice chooses x_{2k+1}=0 (zero strategy), S_{2k+1} = S_{2k} = k sqrt(2). So they compare S_{2k} to λ (2k+1). Indeed they compare S_{2k} (which equals S_{2k+1}) to λ (2k+1). So they claim λ (2k+1) > k sqrt(2). Let's compute λ (2k+1) = (sqrt(2)/2) (2k+1) = sqrt(2) k + sqrt(2)/2. So λ (2k+1) > sqrt(2) k = S_{2k} (since S_{2k} = k sqrt(2)). Indeed sqrt(2) k + sqrt(2)/2 > sqrt(2) k. So the inequality holds for λ = sqrt(2)/2 exactl