ven number,\n   \\[\n   x_{2k}= \\sqrt{\\,2-a_k^{2}\\,}\\qquad(0\\le a_k\\le\\sqrt2).\n   \\tag{A2}\n   \\]\n   The odd‑turn numbers satisfy the one‑dimensional recurrence\n   \\[\n   a_{k+1}=2\\lambda-\\sqrt{\\,2-a_k^{2}\\,},\\qquad a_1=\\lambda .\n   \\tag{A3}\n   \\]\n   *Lemma 1.* For \\(\\lambda>1\\) the sequence \\((a_k)\\) is strictly increasing.  \n   *Lemma 2.* The equation \\(a=2\\lambda-\\sqrt{2-a^{2}}\\) has no real solution when \\(\\lambda>1\\).  \n   *Lemma 3.* Consequently \\((a_k)\\) cannot stay bounded by \\(\\sqrt2\\); it must eventually satisfy \\(a_k>\\sqrt2\\).  \n\n   Proofs of the three lemmas are given in the detailed part.  \n   When \\(a_k>\\sqrt2\\) we have \\(a_k^{2}>2\\) and therefore\n   \\[\n   Q_{2k-1}=2(k-1)+a_k^{2}>2k .\n   \\]\n   At the following even turn Bazza would need a non‑negative \\(x_{2k}\\) with\n   \\(x_{2k}^{2}\\le 2k-Q_{2k-1}<0\\), which is impossible.  Hence Bazza cannot move and Alice wins.  Thus Alice has a winning strategy for every \\(\\lambda>1\\).\n\n3. **The intermediate interval \\(\\displaystyle\\frac{\\sqrt2}{2}\\le\\lambda\\le1\\).**  \n   *Alice cannot force a win.*  \n   With the tight strategy (A1) the recurrence (A3) applies. For \\(\\lambda\\le1\\) the quadratic\n   \\(a=2\\lambda-\\sqrt{2-a^{2}}\\) has the two real solutions\n   \\[\n   a_{\\pm}= \\lambda\\pm\\sqrt{1-\\lambda^{2}},\\qquad 0\\le a_{-}<a_{+}\\le\\sqrt2 .\n   \\]\n   The map \\(f(a)=2\\lambda-\\sqrt{2-a^{2}}\\) satisfies \\(f'(a_{-})\\in(0,1)\\); hence \\(a_{-}\\) is an attracting fixed point. Starting from \\(a_1=\\lambda\\) we obtain a decreasing sequence converging to \\(a_{-}<\\sqrt2\\). Consequently Alice never reaches a value \\(> \\sqrt2\\) and cannot force Bazza’s loss.\n\n   *Bazza cannot force a win.*  \n   Alice can simply keep all her odd moves equal to zero: \\(x_{2k-1}=0\\) for all \\(k\\).  \n   Bazza’s optimal response on each even turn is again (B1), giving \\(x_{2k}= \\sqrt2\\) and\n   \\[\n   S_{2k}=k\\sqrt2 .\n   \\]\n   For \\(\\lambda\\ge\\sqrt2/2\\) we have\n   \\[\n   \\lambda(2k+1)-S_{2k}=k\\bigl(2\\lambda-\\sqrt2\\bigr)+\\lambda\\ge0 ,\n   \\]\n   so Alice never violates her inequality.  Moreover Bazza never violates his, because after each even turn the square‑sum equals exactly \\(2k\\).  Hence Bazza cannot force a win.\n\n   Since neither player can force a win, the game can be continued indefinitely when\n   \\(\\displaystyle\\frac{\\sqrt2}{2}\\le\\lambda\\le1\\).\n\nThe three cases together give the complete classification stated in the boxed answer.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Detailed Solution ###\n\nLet a fixed \\(\\lambda>0\\) be given.  \nFor each turn \\(n\\) define  \n\n\\[\nS_n:=\\sum_{i=1}^{n}x_i ,\\qquad \nQ_n:=\\sum_{i=1}^{n}x_i^{2}.\n\\]\n\nThe rules are  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{cases}\n\\text{if }n\\text{ is odd: } S_n\\le\\lambda n,\\\\[2mm]\n\\text{if }n\\text{ is even: } Q_n\\le n .\n\\end{cases}\\tag{R}\n\\]\n\nA player who cannot choose a legal \\(x_n\\) loses; if the game never ends it is a draw.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  Bazza wins when \\(\\displaystyle\\lambda<\\frac{\\sqrt2}{2}\\)\n\n**Bazza’s strategy.**  \nAssume Alice has just played the odd move \\(x_{2k-1}=t\\) (\\(0\\le t\\le\\sqrt2\\) because otherwise she would already have violated (R) on the previous even turn).  \nBazza chooses  \n\n\\[\nx_{2k}= \\sqrt{\\,2-t^{2}\\,}. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n*Verification of the even‑turn constraint.*  \nBefore his move we have \\(Q_{2k-2}=2(k-1)\\) (induction, see below).  \nAfter Alice’s move the square‑sum is \\(Q_{2k-2}+t^{2}=2(k-1)+t^{2}\\).  \nAdding (1) gives  \n\n\\[\nQ_{2k}=2(k-1)+t^{2}+ \\bigl(2-t^{2}\\bigr)=2k,\n\\]\n\nso the even‑turn inequality \\(Q_{2k}\\le 2k\\) holds with equality.\n\n*Induction basis.*  For \\(k=1\\) we have \\(Q_0=0\\) and after Bazza’s first move \\(Q_2=2\\), as required.\n\n*Growth of the ordinary sum.*  \nDefine  \n\n\\[\ng(t):=t+\\sqrt{2-t^{2}},\\qquad 0\\le t\\le\\sqrt2 .\n\\]\n\nA short calculus check shows \\(g'(t)=1-\\dfrac{t}{\\sqrt{2-t^{2}}}\\) and that the minimum of \\(g\\) on \\([0,\\sqrt2]\\) occurs at the endpoints, where \\(g(0)=g(\\sqrt2)=\\sqrt2\\).  Hence  \n\n\\[\ng(t)\\ge\\sqrt2\\quad\\text{for all }t\\in[0,\\sqrt2]. \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nConsequently after each pair of moves the ordinary sum increases by at least \\(\\sqrt2\\):\n\\[\nS_{2k}=S_{2k-2}+g\\!\\bigl(x_{2k-1}\\bigr)\\ge S_{2k-2}+\\sqrt2 .\n\\]\nIterating gives  \n\n\\[\nS_{2k}\\ge k\\sqrt2\\qquad(k\\ge1). \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n*Eventual violation of Alice’s condition.*  \nBecause \\(\\lambda<\\sqrt2/2\\),\n\n\\[\n\\frac{k\\sqrt2}{\\lambda(2k+1)}\\xrightarrow[k\\to\\infty]{}\\frac{\\sqrt2}{2\\lambda}>1 .\n\\]\n\nHence there exists an integer \\(K\\) such that for all \\(k\\ge K\\)\n\n\\[\nk\\sqrt2>\\lambda(2k+1). \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nWhen (4) holds we already have \\(S_{2k}>\\lambda(2k+1)\\) at the *beginning* of Alice’s next odd turn.  No non‑negative \\(x_{2k+1}\\) can make the inequality \\(S_{2k}+x_{2k+1}\\le\\lambda(2k+1)\\) true, so Alice is unable to move and loses.  \n\nThus Bazza’s strategy (1) guarantees a win for every \\(\\lambda<\\sqrt2/2\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Alice wins when \\(\\displaystyle\\lambda>1\\)\n\n**Alice’s strategy (tight sum).**  \nOn each odd turn \\(2k-1\\) she chooses\n\n\\[\nx_{2k-1}= \\lambda(2k-1)-S_{2k-2}. \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nThus after her move  \n\n\\[\nS_{2k-1}= \\lambda(2k-1). \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\na_k:=x_{2k-1}\\qquad(k\\ge1).\n\\]\n\n**Bazza’s optimal reply.**  \nGiven \\(a_k\\) (which satisfies \\(0\\le a_k\\le\\sqrt2\\) because otherwise the even‑turn constraint would already be violated), the largest even‑turn number he can legally pick is  \n\n\\[\nx_{2k}= \\sqrt{\\,2-a_k^{2}\\,}. \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nWith this choice the even‑turn inequality is tight:\n\\(Q_{2k}=2k\\).\n\n**Recurrence for the odd‑turn numbers.**  \nFrom (5) and (7),\n\n\\[\na_{k+1}=x_{2k+1}= \\lambda(2k+1)-S_{2k}\n        =\\lambda(2k+1)-\\bigl(\\lambda(2k-1)+a_k+\\sqrt{2-a_k^{2}}\\bigr)\n        =2\\lambda-\\sqrt{2-a_k^{2}}. \\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nHence  \n\n\\[\na_{k+1}=f(a_k),\\qquad f(x)=2\\lambda-\\sqrt{2-x^{2}},\\qquad a_1=\\lambda . \\tag{9}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### Lemma 1 (Monotonicity).  \nFor \\(\\lambda>1\\) the sequence \\((a_k)\\) defined by (9) is strictly increasing.\n\n*Proof.*  \nBecause \\(0\\le a_k\\le\\sqrt2\\), we have \\(\\sqrt{2-a_k^{2}}\\le\\sqrt2\\).  Thus  \n\n\\[\na_{k+1}=2\\lambda-\\sqrt{2-a_k^{2}}\\ge 2\\lambda-\\sqrt2>1 .\n\\]\n\nNow compute the difference\n\n\\[\na_{k+1}-a_k\n=2\\lambda-\\sqrt{2-a_k^{2}}-a_k\n=\\lambda-\\bigl(\\sqrt{2-a_k^{2}}-(\\lambda-a_k)\\bigr).\n\\]\n\nSince \\(\\lambda>1\\) and \\(a_k\\le\\sqrt2<\\lambda+1\\), the term in parentheses is **strictly smaller** than \\(\\lambda-a_k\\); consequently the whole expression is positive, i.e. \\(a_{k+1}>a_k\\). ∎\n\n\n\n##### Lemma 2 (No fixed point).  \nThe equation \\(a=2\\lambda-\\sqrt{2-a^{2}}\\) has no real solution when \\(\\lambda>1\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nSquaring gives  \n\n\\[\na^{2}=4\\lambda^{2}-4\\lambda\\sqrt{2-a^{2}}+2-a^{2}\n\\;\\Longrightarrow\\;\n2a^{2}=4\\lambda^{2}+2-4\\lambda\\sqrt{2-a^{2}} .\n\\]\n\nIsolating the square‑root and squaring again yields the quadratic  \n\n\\[\na^{2}-2\\lambda a+(2\\lambda^{2}-1)=0 .\n\\]\n\nIts discriminant is \\(\\Delta=4(1-\\lambda^{2})<0\\) for \\(\\lambda>1\\); therefore no real root exists. ∎\n\n\n\n##### Lemma 3 (Escape beyond \\(\\sqrt2\\)).  \nFor \\(\\lambda>1\\) the increasing sequence \\((a_k)\\) cannot stay bounded by \\(\\sqrt2\\); consequently there exists \\(k\\) with \\(a_k>\\sqrt2\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nAssume, to the contrary, that \\(a_k\\le\\sqrt2\\) for all \\(k\\).  Then \\((a_k)\\) is monotone increasing and bounded, so it converges to some limit \\(L\\le\\sqrt2\\).  Passing to the limit in the recurrence (9) gives \\(L=2\\lambda-\\sqrt{2-L^{2}}\\), i.e. \\(L\\) would be a real fixed point of \\(f\\).  By Lemma 2 such a fixed point does not exist, a contradiction. ∎\n\n\n\n**Conclusion of Alice’s win.**  \nLet \\(k\\) be the first index with \\(a_k>\\sqrt2\\).  Then  \n\n\\[\nQ_{2k-1}=Q_{2k-2}+a_k^{2}=2(k-1)+a_k^{2}>2k .\n\\]\n\nAt the following even turn Bazza would need a non‑negative \\(x_{2k}\\) satisfying  \n\n\\[\nx_{2k}^{2}\\le 2k-Q_{2k-1}<0,\n\\]\n\nwhich is impossible.  Hence Bazza cannot move and loses.  Therefore Alice’s tight‑sum strategy (5) is a winning strategy for every \\(\\lambda>1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  The interval \\(\\displaystyle\\frac{\\sqrt2}{2}\\le\\lambda\\le1\\)\n\n**(i) Alice cannot force a win.**  \nIf Alice follows the tight‑sum strategy (5), the odd‑turn numbers obey the recurrence (9).  \nFor \\(\\lambda\\le1\\) the quadratic from Lemma 2 has the two real roots  \n\n\\[\na_{\\pm}= \\lambda\\pm\\sqrt{1-\\lambda^{2}},\\qquad\n0\\le a_{-}<a_{+}\\le\\sqrt2 .\n\\]\n\nThe map \\(f\\) satisfies \\(f(a_{-})=a_{-}\\) and \\(0<f'(a_{-})<1\\); thus \\(a_{-}\\) is an attracting fixed point.  \nStarting from \\(a_1=\\lambda\\) we obtain a decreasing sequence converging to \\(a_{-}<\\sqrt2\\).  Consequently Alice never reaches a value \\(> \\sqrt2\\); by the same argument as in the previous section Bazza always has a legal move, so Alice cannot force a win.\n\n**(ii) Bazza cannot force a win.**  \nAlice can simply keep all her odd moves equal to zero:\n\\[\nx_{2k-1}=0\\qquad(k\\ge1).\n\\]\nGiven such a move, Bazza’s maximal admissible response is again (1) with \\(t=0\\), i.e. \\(x_{2k}= \\sqrt2\\).  \nHence after \\(k\\) pairs  \n\n\\[\nS_{2k}=k\\sqrt2,\\qquad Q_{2k}=2k .\n\\]\n\nFor any \\(\\lambda\\ge\\sqrt2/2\\),\n\n\\[\n\\lambda(2k+1)-S_{2k}\n= k\\bigl(2\\lambda-\\sqrt2\\bigr)+\\lambda\\ge0 .\n\\]\n\nThus Alice never violates her odd‑turn inequality, and Bazza never violates his even‑turn inequality (the square‑sum is exactly at the bound).  Therefore Bazza cannot force a win.\n\nSince neither player possesses a forced‑win strategy, the game can be continued indefinitely when \\(\\displaystyle\\frac{\\sqrt2}{2}\\le\\lambda\\le1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Final classification\n\nCombining the three cases we obtain\n\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{c|c}\n\\lambda & \\text{outcome} \\\\ \\hline\n\\lambda<\\dfrac{\\sqrt2}{2} & \\text{Bazza has a winning strategy} \\\\[2mm]\n\\dfrac{\\sqrt2}{2}\\le\\lambda\\le1 & \\text{no player has a forced win (the game may last forever)}\\\\[2mm]\n\\lambda>1 & \\text{Alice has a winning strategy}\n\\end{array}\n\\]\n\nwhich is exactly the statement boxed in the summary.  \n\n--- \n\n**Answer.**  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\n\\begin{aligned}\n&\\text{Alice wins } \\iff \\lambda>1,\\\\[2mm]\n&\\text{Bazza wins } \\iff \\lambda<\\dfrac{\\sqrt2}{2},\\\\[2mm]\n&\\text{If }\\dfrac{\\sqrt2}{2}\\le\\lambda\\le1\\text{ the game can be played forever.}\n\\end{aligned}}\n\\]"
